 "meta_desc": "Class 11 Sequences and Series worksheet (NCERT Chapter 8): G.P. nth term, common ratio, sum of n terms, infinite G.P., A.M., G.M., inserting means, A.M. ≥ G.M. and word problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "In a G.P., aₙ = arⁿ⁻¹ and Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1).",
 "key_sub": "For |r| &lt; 1, the sum to infinity is a/(1 − r).",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><ul><li>A <b>G.P.</b> multiplies by the same ratio r: a, ar, ar², …; a<sub>n</sub> = ar<sup>n−1</sup>.</li><li>Sum of n terms: S<sub>n</sub> = a(r<sup>n</sup> − 1) ÷ (r − 1) (r ≠ 1). If |r| &lt; 1, S<sub>∞</sub> = a ÷ (1 − r).</li><li>A.M. of a and b = (a + b) ÷ 2; G.M. = √(ab). For positive a, b: A.M. ≥ G.M.</li></ul><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find the 10th term of 5, 25, 125, …</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>a = 5, r = 5</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>a₁₀ = 5 × 5⁹ = 5¹⁰</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 5¹⁰ = 97,65,625</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Using arⁿ instead of arⁿ⁻¹ for the nth term.</li><li>Finding a sum to infinity when |r| ≥ 1.</li><li>Using (a + b)/2 for the G.M.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "When does an infinite G.P. have a sum?",
   "a": "Only when −1 &lt; r &lt; 1."
  },
  {
   "q": "Is the A.M. always at least the G.M.?",
   "a": "Yes, for two positive numbers; they are equal only when the numbers are equal."
  }
